course: everyday
lesson: 457
title: The cat that only likes Dad, and other choosy pets
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Chloe's family gets a rescue cat called Duchess. Duchess sleeps on Dad's chest, purrs on Dad's lap and sits by the door at six waiting for Dad's key. When Chloe reaches for her, Duchess flattens her ears and stalks off like a duchess who's been insulted. Chloe chases her for cuddles, and Duchess hides under the bed for a whole day. Chloe even puts up a poster saying «Duchess loves Chloe»; that night Duchess sleeps on Dad's lap right underneath it. Then Chloe takes over the feeding for a fortnight, and slowly Duchess starts winding round her legs at teatime — before going straight back to Dad's lap.
explain: Choosy pets nearly always have a reason. Sam's hamster Nugget bites because Sam's hand comes down from above like a hawk; when Mum shows him how to let Nugget walk onto a flat palm from the side, the biting stops. Connor's cat Sooty avoids him because he plays his drum kit next to her basket; he moves the drums to the garage and a week later she sits on his bed. Maddie's dog Pickles goes stiff around her new stepdad's deep voice and big boots, until three months of morning walks win him over.
explain: Some animals bite back hard. Little Alfie grabs Duchess's tail and gets scratched across the hand — Duchess said no the only way a cat can. Nan's parrot Captain says «Hello gorgeous» to Nan and «Shut it» to everyone else; Jake pokes his finger through the bars to teach it «Jake is boss» and gets a bite that throbs all evening. Grandad's sheepdog Bess nips Chloe's heels, herding her like a lost lamb, until Grandad whistles her off.
explain: In Glasgow, Lily's auntie's cat Bawheid ignores her all weekend. «Och, he's a crabbit wee thing, dinnae take it tae heart, hen,» says her auntie — crabbit is Scots for grumpy, wee for small, dinnae for don't, and hen is a friendly «love». On the last morning Bawheid sits on Lily's suitcase so she can't pack. «That's the nicest thing he's ever done,» says her auntie. Some animals say they like you in odd small ways.
fact: Cats often choose a favourite person, usually the calm one who feeds them and doesn't chase them.
fact: Chasing a pet or grabbing it for cuddles makes it trust you less; sitting still and letting it come to you works better.
fact: Animals come to like the people who do the daily jobs: feeding, water, cleaning, quiet company.
fact: Cats scratch and pets bite when they are hurt or scared; the fix is to change how you touch them, not to punish them.
fact: Grabbing a cat's tail hurts it, and it may scratch hard.
fact: A small pet like a hamster may be frightened by a hand coming down from above, as a hawk would; let it walk onto your flat hand from the side.
fact: Loud noise near a pet's bed, like drums or a blaring telly, can make it avoid you.
fact: Never poke your fingers into a bird's cage: a parrot's beak can bite badly.
fact: Animals can take weeks or months to trust a new person in the house, and patient daily care usually wins them over.
fact: Herding dogs like sheepdogs may nip at running children's heels from instinct; the owner should call the dog off.
fact: Some animals show they like you in small ways, like sitting on your things, rather than with big cuddles.
fact: In Scotland «crabbit» means grumpy, «wee» means small, «dinnae» means don't, and «hen» is a friendly word for a girl or woman.
exercise: Why does Duchess choose Dad? | He is calm and he is the one who feeds her | He is the tallest in the family, so she feels safe | He smells of fish all the time | Cats always pick the oldest man
exercise: What happened when Chloe chased Duchess for cuddles? | Duchess hid under the bed all day | Duchess finally purred on her lap | Duchess followed her to school | Duchess went back to the rescue
exercise: What helped Duchess start to like Chloe? | Chloe doing the feeding for two weeks | Chloe calling her name louder every day | Chloe putting up a big poster of Duchess | Chloe picking her up and cuddling her every hour
exercise: Why did Duchess scratch Alfie? | He grabbed her tail and hurt her | She was bored of sitting still on the sofa | Cats scratch toddlers for fun | She wanted his biscuit
exercise: After a pet scratches a child who hurt it, what's the right fix? | Teach the child to touch it gently | Shut the cat outside for a week | Shout at the cat until it learns | Give the cat back to the rescue
exercise: Why did Jake's finger end up throbbing? | He poked it into the parrot's cage | The parrot flew out of its cage at his face | He caught it in the cage door | Nan smacked his hand away
exercise: Why does Nugget the hamster bite Sam? | A hand from above looks like a hawk to him | Hamsters hate the smell of boys' hands | Nugget is broken and needs replacing by the shop | Hamsters bite everyone forever
exercise: How should you pick up a hamster? | Let it walk onto your flat hand from the side | Grab it quickly from above, before it can run | Lift it by the scruff of its neck | Shake the cage till it comes out
exercise: Why did Sooty avoid Connor? | He played his drums next to her basket | Cats can tell who is the youngest | Connor smelled of the neighbour's dog | Sooty only likes people in uniforms
exercise: How did Maddie's stepdad win Pickles over? | Morning walks and patient hellos for months | Shouting «sit» until Pickles obeyed him at last | Buying Pickles a new collar | Ignoring him until he gave up
exercise: Why did Bess nip at Chloe's heels? | Herding is her instinct, and Chloe ran like a lamb | Bess was angry with Chloe for coming to the farm | Bess was hungry for shoes | Bess was trying to play fetch
exercise: «He's a crabbit wee thing, dinnae take it tae heart.» What is the auntie saying? | He's a grumpy little thing, don't take it personally | He's a crab, keep him off your heart | He's very ill and weak, so please don't ever touch him | He loves you, give him a hug
exercise: What did Bawheid sitting on Lily's suitcase show? | His odd way of saying goodbye | That he wanted to be packed and taken | That he hated suitcases and bags | That he was cold and wanted a bed
exercise: What usually makes a pet like you best? | Looking after it every day | Buying it the most toys and treats | Hugging it whenever you want | Being the loudest in the house
exercise: Chloe's «Duchess loves Chloe» poster went up. Where did Duchess sleep? | On Dad's lap right underneath it | On the poster itself, after pulling it down | On Chloe's pillow at last | In Chloe's school bag
test: «get rid of the hamster, it's defective lol». What should Sam do instead? | Change how he picks it up | Swap it for a goldfish instead | Wear gloves and grab harder | Put it outside to toughen up
test: Alfie screams «bad cat!» after grabbing her tail. Is the cat bad? | No, she was hurt and said no the only way she can | Yes, a good cat should never scratch, whatever happens | Yes, she should be punished | No, Alfie scratched himself
test: «she's only nice to dad cos he gives her tuna, it's well fake». What's the truth about pets? | Animals love whoever cares for them daily, and that's real | Cats can only love one person forever | Tuna is the only thing cats love | Pets never love anyone at all
test: Jake: «that parrot needs teaching who's boss». What would most likely happen if he keeps poking it? | It bites him harder, and learns nothing | It learns to say «Jake is boss» after a week | It becomes his favourite person | It falls asleep from boredom
test: A film shows a cat jumping into a new kid's arms on day one. What is real life usually like? | The cat watches from far away for weeks | Every cat jumps into new arms on the very first day | Cats only like kids with films | The cat will leave the house forever
test: «my new stepdad says the dog hates him». What's most likely true? | The dog needs time to get used to him | The dog will never change its mind about him | Dogs know who they hate from birth | The stepdad should get rid of it
test: Connor: «sooty's got beef with me, it's personal». What's really going on? | She hates the noise of his drums near her bed | She is jealous of his sister and wants her to herself | She can tell he's a boy | She's just a mean cat
test: You visit your auntie in Glasgow and her cat ignores you. She says «och, dinnae fret». What should you do? | Leave the cat be and let it come if it wants | Chase it round the flat till it gives in to you | Hide its food so it needs you | Tell your auntie her cat is broken
test: Grandad's sheepdog starts circling and nipping your little cousin who's running about. Best move? | Get Grandad to call the dog off | Let them carry on, it's a game | Shout at the dog and hit it | Tell your cousin to run faster
test: Which of these is most likely to make a shy cat trust you? | Sitting quietly and letting it come to you | Picking it up and cuddling it as often as you can | Following it into every room | Holding it so it can't escape
test: «chloe made a poster so now duchess HAS to love her». Does that work? | No, pets don't love on command | Yes, cats can read posters, everyone knows | Yes, if it has a picture of fish | No, she should make two posters
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a domestic cat may exhibit a preference for one member of a household. | That member provides calm and regular care, including feeding | That member is the eldest person in the dwelling | Cats are compelled to prefer male persons | The preference is determined at the cat's birth
exam: Given that a small rodent persistently bites when lifted from above, ascertain the most appropriate remedy. | To permit the animal to walk onto a flat palm from the side | To return the animal to the vendor on the ground of its being defective | To lift the animal more swiftly from above | To wear thick gloves and grip the animal firmly
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a cat that scratches a child who has pulled its tail? | The cat has responded to pain and fear | The cat is of a malicious disposition | The cat seeks to dominate the household | The cat has acted entirely without any cause
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the acceptance of a new household member by a resident dog. | It may require months of patient daily care | It occurs immediately in all cases | It can never occur once the dog is adult | It depends solely upon the purchase of gifts
